In this article, the people testing this recognize that using Wi-Fi fingerprints (such as RSS)  for device-free passive indoor localization is undermined by inconsistencies in the measured signal environment.
Some examples of this are fluctuations in signal strength due to movement, multipath effects, body shadowing, environmental changes, and time-varying channel conditions. 
To mitigate this, they propose a method that first models the fingerprint data in a way that captures and filters out the high-variance components. 
They apply a specific statistical filtering approach to suppress those fingerprint measurements that deviate strongly from the typical signal pattern at a given location.
After that part is done, they refine the fingerprint matching scheme by incorporating temporal stability constraints. 
Instead of treating each fingerprint measurement on their own, they compare online measurements to a stable baseline that has been collected after filtering.
This helps reduce the impact of transient signal fluctuations. 
Finally, their localization algorithm selects only those access point measurements or sub-channels whose signal behaviour has been shown to be consistent over time. 
By doing so, they reduce the mismatch between offline reference fingerprints and online measurements, yielding improved localisation accuracy and robustness in the face of signal inconsistency.
